stock prices immediately sold off after  news broke that russia had invaded  ukraine and oil prices rose to more than  100 per barrel markets are moving up and  down as traders seek out new information  but you may be wondering why a conflict  in eastern europe would take the value  of u.s companies or raise the price of  gas for american consumers i'll detail  how global conflicts move the stock  market why the possibility of war in  ukraine has had such a strong impact on  oil prices and why this extreme  volatility might not last  stocks are considered a risky investment  because they are pieces of companies and  companies are dependent on customers  when customers are scared they tend to  buy less and reduced buying has a ripple  effect throughout an economy conflicts  like the one in ukraine can make people  around the world nervous because they  touch so many powerful and nuclear armed  nations  it's not just russia and ukraine the  united states china and much of the  european union are involved in this  conflict now markets are forward-looking  so even before it actually happens  investors anticipate that consumers will  pull back on spending that will hurt  companies large and small and because of  the global nature of the conflict you  could see impacts just about everywhere  so traders sell stocks this has become  something of a knee-jerk reaction  especially as computer-generated  algorithms drive larger chunks of the  market the algos read through millions  of headlines and make trades accordingly  in milliseconds based on programmed  expectations but we're seeing oil prices  move in the opposite direction even  though it's a historically risky asset  that's because what's more important to  the price of oil than risk is supply and  demand even if demand slows down because  consumers are fearful a conflict in  ukraine could have a much bigger impact  on supply russia is one of the world's  largest petroleum exporters it accounts  for 18 of the world's natural gas and 12  percent of its oil and much of that  flows through pipelines in ukraine so a  conflict between the two countries could  have a notable impact traders are also  worried about sanctions from the u.s  europe and others the u.s state  department has said its sanctions won't  target energy markets but that could  change  and russia could cut its supply in  response to other sanctions oil prices  also have a big impact on other  commodities most commodities are pulled  from the source and put on trucks then  on airplanes or ships all those things  run on oil so higher oil prices mean  higher delivery prices which feeds into  the overall cost that's why you've seen  the big moves in stocks and commodities  so far but this may not last in recent  years markets have been less responsive  to geopolitical tension that's partially  because consumers have been resilient in  the face of global conflict over much  the past decade it's also because  central banks have stepped in to push  enormous sums of money into economies  that has helped buoy companies during  times of stress and also distorted the  normal supply and demand function that  markets provide but with inflation  rising around the world central banks  may not be able to step in as they have  in the past companies and investors will  be carefully watching the conflict and  consumer sentiment but they will also be  watching how the federal reserve  european central bank and others respond  while initial reactions are pretty easy  to predict we are witnessing a new  environment for the market and the world  we are in unprecedented times and the  market will be moving accordingly  if you enjoyed this video don't forget  to hit the like button and subscribe to  this channel for my latest videos on  markets  in the words the king of the south ti  life is like a chess move you need to  make your next move your best move i'll  see you next time  you
 

